[Music]  hello and welcome to my talk on flimsy  feminism  i hope there are men listening as well  as women because that's  part of what i'm talking about what do i  mean by  flimsy feminism what i mean is that  a lot of us almost all of us i hope now  believe that women are in every respect  equal to men that they are just as  competent  just as intelligent just as expert as  men but do we  actually put those beliefs into practice  in our everyday interactions with women  even something as  trivial as you see a man and a woman  standing together  and you go up to talk to them and you  just automatically address the man first  now that's a very small example but i'm  going to talk about the ways in which  we don't really practice what we preach  or what we believe  when it comes to feminism here's a  classic example of it  which i think a lot of women will  recognize doesn't that happen so often  you're an expert in something you talk  to a man about it  and he tells you what is actually the  case okay it's the offer name is  mansplaining  now i call this the authority gap we all  know about the gender  pay gap and all sorts of other gaps  between men and women but this is  possibly the mother of all gaps the  authority gap  and it's the implicit assumption that a  man knows what he's talking about until  he proves otherwise  while so often for women it's the other  way around  so define my terms authority what do i  do i immediately look at the oxford  online dictionary and i do a screenshot  for you  and it tells us that authority is about  having power over other people  and also being an authority on a subject  in other words having expertise  but what killed me when i looked at this  and it wasn't what i was looking for in  the first place  was that every single example they give  begins with a pronoun  he so we have he had absolute authority  over his subordinates  he has the natural authority of one who  is used to being obeyed  he hit the ball with authority and he  was an authority on the stock market  i mean as if margaret thatcher didn't  have the natural authority of one who  was used to being obeyed or serena  williams doesn't hit the ball with  authority or  helena morrissey isn't an expert on  authority on the stock market i mean  sometimes your subject just  comes up and slaps you in the face how  we associate  men with authority and how we are much  more uncomfortable  associating women with authority and  that is  a very uh important example of the  flimsy feminism i'm  trying to illustrate here so in the  course of researching the book that i'm  writing  which is going to be called the  authority gap i have interviewed about  50 women who are  right at the very very top of their game  so the most authoritative women i could  come across  and you might ask yourself well why is  she talking to them what have they got  to teach  us about this lack of respect for  women's authority and  my thinking was if even they have  experienced the authority gap  if they even they have experienced these  phenomena of not being respected as much  not being listened to as much being  ignored being interrupted  being talked over and surely that proves  that it's true  for the rest of womankind so here's an  example francis morris she is  one of the most powerful not just women  in the art world but people in the art  world globally  she's director of tate modern and she  said to me as director of tate modern  i can spend all day as a powerful  articulate person who's taken seriously  and i can leave this building and i'm  nobody because there's a woman in the  world out there  i'm not taken seriously i'm very often  in situations where people who don't  know my job  and my hand is shaken after the hand of  a male colleague  my eyes are met after the eyes of my  husband are met and no interest is shown  in my opinion  if it's just my opinion as a late  middle-aged female  and i see all that because it's in stark  contrast with the way i'm approached and  treated when it's known that i'm the  director of tank mode  so of course i said to her well maybe  that happens to male directors of tate  modern too if people don't know what  their job is  oh no she said i've worked for three  male directors of tate martin  and it hasn't happened to them i think  that's a fantastic example  of the authority gap now  i'm sure a lot of women have had this  experience you're at a meeting  you come up with a suggestion nobody  takes a blind bit of notice  until a man makes exactly the same  suggestion and everyone says brilliant  idea  should be rao i talked to she's former  treasurer of alphabet which is the  parent company of google  big job right and she said you have to  constantly prove yourself over and over  again  when a guy says something at a meeting  the others will say yeah that sounds  great i think we should do it  and a woman says something they'll say  oh well i think we should look into it  i think we need more data i think we  need to think about that i don't know if  it's really going to work  she says i get challenged a lot  constantly and it's become a way of life  now one form that the authority gap  takes  is being interrupted so you are an  authority on a subject  and somebody usually a man i'm afraid to  say and this isn't prejudice this is  that  simply interrupts you and talks over you  now that is an incredible it's  incredibly undermining of your authority  first of all it suggests that he thinks  what he's got to say is much more  interesting than what you've got to say  and secondly it is literally silencing  you if he's talking over you  so here was a study done by some social  psychologists and they looked at  31 conversations a third between two men  a third between two women and the third  between a man and a woman  and in the first two put together there  were only seven interruptions  in the third one the mixed sex group  there were 48  46 of them by a man now  i mean this is obviously incredibly  striking but it is quite an old study  it's several decades old  so i thought i wonder if that's still  the case so i then found a study done  only a few years ago of 10 000 hours of  oral  transcripts of oral proceedings in the  u.s supreme court  you don't get more authoritative than  being a us supreme court justice right  here's the fabulous  ruth bader ginsburg it turns out that  women make up only a third of the  justices  that have to suffer two-thirds of all  interruptions so in other words they are  four times more likely to be interrupted  than the men on the supreme court  and 96 of those interruptions were by  men  now another interesting facet of this is  talking time  now the urban myth is oh women talk much  more than men do  actually it's not true if you like  somebody up for the day  and you measure men and women how many  words they say in the course of the day  it's pretty much the same but it is  certainly true that  in public when it comes to sort of  expatiating  men tend to bang on for longer than  women i often call it conversational man  spreading  um there was a lovely example here where  researchers gave groups of men and women  um a cassette to talk into so yes  slightly old-fashioned  and three paintings of jura to look at  and then they were asked to talk for as  long as they liked about what they saw  and liked or disliked about these  paintings  on average women talked for 3.17 minutes  men talked on average for four times as  long 13 minutes  but even that wasn't accurate because  three of the men were still talking when  their cassette tapes and 30-minute  cassette tapes  ran out um but i think a possibly a more  interesting study was done  where the researchers put men and women  into small groups of i think eight  people in each group and they were asked  to come up with a solution to a sort of  town hall problem about whether to raise  taxes to spend more money on local  services  and they found that in almost every um  mixture of men and women so they they  had groups that were all men  one woman two women three women four  women or women  in almost every group the women talked  disproportionately less than the men  and for a disproportionate amount of  time so if they made up one-fifth of the  group they talked for less than  one-fifth  of the time except for in two  circumstances one was if women made up  at least 80 percent of the group  and then women talked for a  proportionate amount of time  but interestingly the second um reason  was when the rules of decision making  would change  so that instead of having to have a  majority agreeing  on the solution had to be everybody it  was unanimity so it was like a jury  trial  and in those cases women spoke up now  what that suggests  is that they only spoke up when they  knew their views had to be listened to  because everyone had to agree on the end  solution  and they knew that otherwise what was  the point frankly  now were they right to be  uh suspicious of whether their views  would be taken into account  i'm very sorry to say that they were  because here's a very interesting study  on influence  bear with me because it's a little bit  complicated but anyway  143 business studies students  roughly half and half male and female  they were given a video to watch and  some  information to read about what to do in  the case of a bushfire  and then they were asked to rank in  order of importance  which objects they should take with them  if they were to make them more likely to  survive  a bushfire and it might have been a  torch or a blanket or whatever  now there is an official ranking of  order of importance for these items  and so their answers were measured  against the official ranking to see  how well they did and how many of these  they sort of got right  it turned out women were just as good at  men as men doing this task  and they were actually just as confident  of their ability as men  and then the researchers put them into  groups and in each group they put one  very high scoring person who they deemed  to be an expert  now the group didn't know who that  expert was and the expert didn't know  who they were  what was the result women exerted less  influence within their groups than men  so in other words if you compared the  women's initial ranking with the ranking  the group finally coalesced on  there was more disparity between the  women's and the men's  female experts were perceived as less  expert  than female non-experts and they exerted  significantly  less influence than the non-experts  which sounds really weird doesn't it  but i'll explain why whereas the male  experts were significantly  more influential than the male  non-experts  to continue watching this video click  the link in the top left or in the  description below  or visit iai.tv for more debates and  talks from the world's leading thinkers  on today's biggest ideas  you

